!id includeprotocols
include protocols buttons should include them to the copied text when copy all button is clicked

!id pasteing
every paste should be sent to protocol manager. protocol manager will decide what to do with it. Minimal coupling with protocol system and the rest of app

!id storage-location
protocols should be held in project root by default, with the ability to change the storage directory (configurable location, not hardcoded to root)

!id rss-fallback
RSS fallback: when ntfy delivery fails after retry, the message is written as an RSS item to KV (key `rss:items`, capped to CONFIG.RSS_MAX_ITEMS, newest first). Served at GET /rss.xml as a valid RSS 2.0 feed. A password-gated "Send test RSS item" button on the index page (GET /__test-rss) adds a synthetic item without requiring an actual ntfy failure. RSS is fallback-only — it does not fire on successful ntfy sends.
On every ntfy failure, deliverMessage reports the RSS fallback outcome (written ok / failed with error / not attempted) rather than swallowing it. The dispatcher logs this outcome alongside the existing send-failure log line, and /__send-now includes it in its response body, so an RSS write failure is never silent.
The RSS feed is reset (all items cleared) once per day, at the point the daily plan is first created in ensureTodayPlan — i.e. before the morning's first scheduled message can fire. Reset only happens on new-plan creation, not on every read of an existing plan, so a mid-day dispatcher self-heal call does not wipe the feed again.
Each RSS item's title is the full oracle message itself, untruncated, so a feed reader notification surfaces the message text directly without opening the item. The item's description holds only the fallback reason ("ntfy delivery failed" or "manual test"), not a repeat of the message — title and description are deliberately non-redundant.
A password-gated "Erase all RSS except last" button on the index page (GET /__trim-rss) manually keeps only the single most recent RSS item, discarding the rest. Distinct from the daily automatic reset in ensureTodayPlan, which clears the feed entirely — this is a manual, user-triggered partial trim.

!id runtime-config
Step 10: scheduling parameters (window start/end, min gap, soft band low/high, max gap ceiling, max messages per day, continuation probability) are runtime-editable from the index page rather than hardcoded. Defaults live in config.js as before; edits are stored as overrides in KV (key `config:overrides`) via runtime-config.js, which merges them over the defaults at read time through getEffectiveConfig(env). All scheduling code (scheduler.js, status.js) must read tunables through getEffectiveConfig rather than importing CONFIG's scheduling fields directly.
GET /api/config returns current effective editable values. POST /api/config (password-protected) validates and saves overrides — validation enforces MIN_GAP_MINUTES \u2264 SOFT_BAND_LOW \u2264 SOFT_BAND_HIGH \u2264 MAX_GAP_CEILING, a fitting window, and sane ranges, rejecting bad combinations before they reach KV.
Saved changes apply only to future plan generation by default. A password-protected "Regenerate today's plan now" button (GET /__regenerate-plan) force-overwrites today's already-generated plan immediately using current effective config, and — via the existing ensureTodayPlan reset path — also clears the RSS feed as part of that regeneration.
The index page password field has a Show/Hide toggle button to reveal typed characters.

